meta {
  name: Evaluate Symbol
  type: http
  seq: 3
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/llm/evaluate/AAPL?timeframe=M5&count=300
  body: none
  auth: none
}

params:query {
  timeframe: M5
  count: 300
  ~sessions: RTH
}

headers {
  Accept: application/json
}

docs {
  # LLM Evaluate Symbol

  The "do it all" analysis endpoint. You give it a symbol + timeframe; the app
  fetches the historical bars, computes the Supertrend series, DISTILS them into
  a compact briefing, and asks the LLM for a verdict AND a forward-looking read
  of the next likely directions.

  Unlike "Analyze Clean Setup" (which takes hand-typed facts), here the briefing
  is built from REAL market data — no manual facts.

  ## Path
  - `{ticker}` — any US symbol (need not be in the watchlist).

  ## Query
  - `timeframe` — M1/M5/M15/M30/M60/M120/M240 or synthetic M2/M3 (default M5).
  - `count`     — bars to fetch (default 300; broker caps at 1650).
  - `sessions`  — RTH/PRE/ATH/OVN (default: configured trading sessions).

  ## Why distilled, not raw bars
  A small local model reasons poorly over hundreds of raw OHLCV rows. The app
  does the quant work (bars + Supertrend) in Java and hands the model a short
  list of facts: current price, window travel, high/low, Supertrend direction +
  line + price-to-line separation in ATR units, the latest flip and its age, the
  flip count, the volume trend, and a tail of the most recent bars. You can see
  exactly what the model saw in `briefingFacts`.

  ## Response (200)
  { ticker, timeframe, sessions, barsFetched, model, llmEnabled,
    briefingFacts: [ ...the distilled facts fed to the LLM... ],
    verdict: {
      action (ENTER|WAIT|SKIP), conviction, rationale, riskFlags[],
      nextDirections: [ { direction (BULLISH|BEARISH|SIDEWAYS), trigger,
                          rationale, likelihood (HIGH|MEDIUM|LOW) } ],
      ok, note
    },
    timestamp }

  - `ok=false` → fail-safe WAIT (LLM disabled/down/bad output); `note` says why.
    The briefing is still returned so you can see the inputs.

  ## Errors (mirrors /api/supertrend)
  - `400` — invalid timeframe/sessions.
  - `502` — market-data (broker) request failed.
  - `503` — no data, or not enough bars for the Supertrend length.

  ## Advisory only
  The verdict and scenarios are opinions — nothing here places an order. The
  risk/exit layer keeps final authority.
}
